::STAT_U01_L01_Q01::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L01_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
A survey asks students how many siblings they have. What type of data is collected?
{
~A. Categorical, because students are being grouped#The variable is the number of siblings, not a group label.
=B. Numerical, because the response is a count#Number of siblings is a count, so numerical summaries such as mean or range make sense.
~C. Categorical, because the answers are whole numbers#Whole numbers can still represent numerical counts.
~D. Neither, because family information is not data#Family information can be data when it answers a statistical question.
}

::STAT_U01_L01_Q02::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L01_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
A poll asks students which school club they joined. What type of data is collected?
{
~A. Numerical, because the poll can count students#Counting responses later does not change the original variable.
~B. Numerical, because each student gives one answer#One response per student does not make the variable numerical.
=C. Categorical, because club names are labels#Club names identify groups rather than measured quantities.
~D. Neither, because clubs are not numbers#Data can be categorical even when responses are words.
}

::STAT_U01_L01_Q03::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L01_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
A table lists classroom numbers where students have first period. How should classroom numbers be classified?
{
~A. Numerical, because the values use digits#Digits alone do not make data numerical.
~B. Numerical, because the rooms can be sorted#Sorting labels does not mean the values measure an amount.
~C. Both, because classrooms contain students#The data value itself is the room label, not the number of students.
=D. Categorical, because the numbers identify locations#Classroom numbers are labels, so arithmetic with them would not be meaningful.
}

::STAT_U01_L01_Q04::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L01_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Which display best compares the number of students choosing each lunch option?
{
~A. Histogram#A histogram groups numerical values into intervals, not lunch categories.
~B. Scatterplot#A scatterplot is for relationships between two numerical variables.
~C. Dot plot#A dot plot is usually used for one numerical variable.
=D. Bar chart#A bar chart compares counts across categories.
}

::STAT_U01_L01_Q05::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L01_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
A dot plot shows quiz scores out of 10 for one class. What should you interpret from it?
{
=A. How the numerical scores are distributed#A dot plot shows the spread, clusters, and repeated values for one numerical variable.
~B. Which score category is most popular#Scores are numerical values, not category labels.
~C. The relationship between two variables#A dot plot for one variable does not show a two-variable relationship.
~D. The cause of high scores#A display can show patterns, but not prove cause by itself.
}

::STAT_U01_L01_Q06::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L01_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
A survey asks for each student's ZIP code. What type of data is collected?
{
=A. Categorical, because ZIP codes are location labels#ZIP codes identify places; adding or averaging them is not meaningful.
~B. Numerical, because ZIP codes contain digits#Digit symbols can be used as labels.
~C. Numerical, because ZIP codes can be ordered#Ordering labels does not make them measurements.
~D. Neither, because ZIP codes are too long#Length of a code does not determine the data type.
}

::STAT_U01_L01_Q07::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L01_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Which variable is numerical?
{
~A. Favorite puzzle type#This is a category label.
=B. Time in seconds to finish a puzzle#Time is a measured quantity that can be compared and summarized.
~C. Homeroom teacher name#Names are categorical labels.
~D. Student ID number#An ID number labels a student instead of measuring an amount.
}

::STAT_U01_L01_Q08::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L01_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
A bar chart displays counts for favorite music genres. What does the height of a bar represent?
{
~A. The average of the genre names#Genre names cannot be averaged.
~B. The order in which students answered#Bar height is about count, not response order.
=C. The number of responses in that category#In a category bar chart, bar height represents frequency or count.
~D. A numerical measurement for each song#The display summarizes category counts, not song measurements.
}

::STAT_U01_L01_Q09::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L01_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
A researcher records the temperature each hour during a day. What type of data is temperature?
{
~A. Categorical, because each hour has a label#The variable asked about is temperature, not the hour label.
~B. Categorical, because temperatures can be grouped#Grouping later does not change the original measurement.
~C. Neither, because weather changes#Changing values can still be numerical data.
=D. Numerical, because temperature is measured#Temperature is a measured quantity.
}

::STAT_U01_L01_Q10::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L01_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Which display is most appropriate for one numerical variable such as backpack weight?
{
=A. Histogram#A histogram can show the distribution of one numerical measurement.
~B. Pie chart#A pie chart is for parts of a whole across categories.
~C. Two-way table#A two-way table summarizes two categorical variables.
~D. Segmented bar graph#A segmented bar graph compares categorical relative frequencies.
}

::STAT_U01_L01_Q11::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L01_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
A data display uses categories "walk," "bus," "car," and "bike." What is the variable?
{
~A. Number of students in school#The counts may be shown, but the categories describe transportation.
=B. Method of transportation to school#The categories are possible values of the transportation variable.
~C. Distance traveled to school#Distance would be a numerical variable, not these category labels.
~D. Time spent commuting#Time would be measured in minutes or hours.
}

::STAT_U01_L01_Q12::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L01_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
A teacher records how many problems each student solved correctly. What type of data is collected?
{
~A. Categorical, because correct and incorrect are categories#The recorded variable is the number correct, not whether one problem was correct.
~B. Categorical, because students are in a class#The context does not determine the data type by itself.
=C. Numerical, because it is a count of correct problems#Counts are numerical when arithmetic comparisons are meaningful.
~D. Neither, because grades are private#Privacy does not change whether the data are numerical.
}

::STAT_U01_L01_Q13::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L01_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
A display shows shoe sizes for students. Why is shoe size treated as numerical in this context?
{
~A. It is categorical because shoes come in styles#Style is a different categorical variable.
~B. It is categorical because size numbers are printed on labels#The numbers still represent size on a scale.
~C. It is neither because shoe sizes are not exact lengths#A measurement scale can still be numerical even if it is rounded.
=D. Shoe size represents an ordered measurement scale#Shoe size acts as a numerical measurement that can be compared.
}

::STAT_U01_L01_Q14::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L01_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Which question would produce categorical data?
{
=A. Which phone brand do students use most often?#Phone brand is a group label.
~B. How many minutes do students use a phone daily?#Minutes are numerical.
~C. What is each student's phone battery percentage?#Percentage is numerical.
~D. How many apps are installed on each phone?#Number of apps is a numerical count.
}

::STAT_U01_L01_Q15::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L01_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
A dataset records player jersey numbers. How should the values be classified?
{
~A. Numerical, because jersey numbers are numbers#Numbers can be used as labels.
=B. Categorical, because jersey numbers identify players#Jersey numbers are labels, not quantities to average.
~C. Numerical, because larger jersey numbers mean better performance#The jersey number does not measure performance.
~D. Both, because sports use statistics#The role of the variable determines the type.
}

::STAT_U01_L01_Q16::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L01_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
A histogram groups commute times into intervals. What can be concluded from the display?
{
~A. Which student has each exact commute time#Grouped intervals may not identify exact individual values.
~B. Which transportation method caused delays#The histogram does not include cause or transportation method.
=C. How commute times are distributed across intervals#Histograms show frequency patterns for numerical intervals.
~D. Which category is most popular#Intervals of time are numerical groups, not category names.
}

::STAT_U01_L01_Q17::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L01_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Which pair correctly matches data type and display?
{
=A. Categorical data and bar chart#Bar charts compare category counts.
~B. Categorical data and scatterplot#Scatterplots require two numerical variables.
~C. Numerical data and two-way table only#Numerical data are commonly displayed with dot plots or histograms.
~D. Numerical labels and pie chart always#Numeric-looking labels may be categorical, and display choice depends on context.
}

::STAT_U01_L01_Q18::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L01_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
A display summarizes favorite book genre by count. What is a reasonable interpretation?
{
~A. The tallest bar has the greatest numerical measurement#Genre is categorical, so the bar height is count, not a measured genre value.
~B. The display proves why students like one genre#A display shows counts, not causes.
=C. The category with the tallest bar has the most responses#For counts by category, taller bars represent more responses.
~D. The average genre can be calculated from the labels#Category names cannot be averaged.
}

::STAT_U01_L01_Q19::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L01_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
A data set records rainfall in inches for each day. What type of data is rainfall?
{
=A. Numerical, because rainfall is a measured amount#Inches of rainfall are measured quantities.
~B. Categorical, because days have names#The variable is rainfall, not day name.
~C. Categorical, because weather has types#Rainfall amount is numerical even though weather descriptions can be categorical.
~D. Neither, because rainfall can be zero#Zero can be a valid numerical value.
}

::STAT_U01_L01_Q20::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L01_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Which value is most likely a categorical label rather than a numerical measurement?
{
~A. 14 minutes of reading#Minutes are a measured amount of time.
~B. 14 correct answers#Correct answers are a count.
=C. Bus route 14#The route number identifies a route; it is not a measured amount.
~D. 14 degrees Celsius#Temperature is a measured quantity.
}

::STAT_U01_L01_Q21::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L01_Q21
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
A student says, "The survey answer is a word, so it is not data." What correction is needed?
{
~A. Only numbers can be used in statistics#Statistics also analyzes categorical variables.
=B. Words can be categorical data when they answer a statistical question#Data may be labels, groups, counts, or measurements.
~C. Words become numerical if counted once#Counting responses summarizes categories but does not change the original data type.
~D. Words should be removed from data displays#Categorical displays are built from word labels.
}

::STAT_U01_L01_Q22::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L01_Q22
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Which question would produce numerical data?
{
=A. How many hours did students volunteer last month?#Hours are measured quantities.
~B. Which volunteer site did students choose?#Site is a category.
~C. What type of service did students perform?#Service type is categorical.
~D. Which month did students volunteer?#Month name is categorical in this context.
}

::STAT_U01_L01_Q23::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L01_Q23
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
A pie chart shows the percent of students in each favorite subject category. What does one slice represent?
{
~A. The average score in that subject#A category share is not an average score.
=B. The part of the total group in one category#A pie chart slice represents a category's share of the whole.
~C. The exact response of every student#A pie chart summarizes groups rather than listing individuals.
~D. A relationship between two numerical variables#Pie charts summarize one categorical variable.
}

::STAT_U01_L01_Q24::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L01_Q24
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
A survey asks students to rate lunch from 1 to 5. In this lesson context, how should the rating be treated?
{
~A. Categorical, because lunch is a category#The variable is the rating, not the lunch type.
~B. Neither, because opinions cannot be data#Opinions can be recorded as data.
~C. Categorical, because 1 and 5 are labels only#In a rating scale, the numbers represent ordered levels.
=D. Numerical, because the rating uses ordered numbers to summarize responses#The rating is recorded on a numerical scale for comparison.
}

::STAT_U01_L01_Q25::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L01_Q25
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
Why must context be checked before classifying data?
{
~A. Because all words become numerical in charts#Words usually represent categories unless coded for analysis.
~B. Because display colors determine data type#Color is visual formatting, not the variable type.
~C. Because every data set has exactly one type#A dataset may include multiple variables with different types.
=D. Because numbers may represent either quantities or labels#Context tells whether arithmetic and measurement are meaningful.
}
